course: dictionary
lesson: 12359
title: Crispy and crunchy words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Crispy is a describing word, an adjective. It tells you how something feels or sounds. Look at the words around it to be sure what it means. «The bacon in the pan went crispy and brown.» Bacon does not go crispy if it is soft and wet, so crispy must mean hard and dry on the outside.
explain: Now read this one: «I bit into a crispy chip and heard a loud crunch.» The crunch is the clue. Crispy food breaks with a pleasant crunch when you bite it. Try your meaning in the sentence: "I bit into a hard, dry chip and heard a loud crunch." It still makes sense, so you have found the meaning.
explain: Crispy is often used about food, but not only food. «The leaves were crispy under our boots in autumn.» Dry autumn leaves crackle when you walk on them, so crispy here means dry and brittle. Crispy is also a good word for describing the sound of food, not just how it looks.
fact: Crispy means hard and dry on the outside, and it breaks with a pleasant crunch when you bite it.
fact: «Crispy» is an adjective, so its other forms are crispier and crispiest.
fact: Crispy bacon, crispy chips, crispy duck and crispy batter are all food words you may meet on a menu.
fact: In cooking, food goes crispy when heat drives water out of it.
fact: Crispy leaves are dry leaves, often seen in autumn.
exercise: «The chips were crispy on the outside and soft inside.» What does crispy mean? | Wet and cold all the way through | Sweet and sticky like a toffee | Soft and warm like bread | *Hard and dry on the outside, with a crunch
exercise: Mum says, «Bite into the toast, it should be crispy.» What should happen when you bite it? | *It breaks with a pleasant crunch | It stays exactly the same | It squeaks and bounces back | It melts like ice cream
exercise: Which of these could be called crispy? | A scoop of ice cream | A bowl of runny soup | A cup of warm milk | *Crispy bacon from the pan
test: «The autumn leaves were crispy and crackled under her feet.» What does crispy mean here? | Green and new | Soft as a pillow | Wet and slippery | *Dry and brittle, breaking with a crackle
test: The cook puts the duck in a hot oven, and the skin turns crispy. What has the heat done? | It has made the skin wet and soft | It has turned the skin into ice | *It has driven water out of the skin | It has added water to the skin
exam: Which sentence uses crispy correctly? | The rain was crispy on the window all night. | The sofa was crispy after its bath. | *The toast was crispy and golden, and it crunched when I bit it. | The song was crispy and loud in the hall.
